{
  "dom_id": "step-q64617",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
